    h1: "How Is Hospital HVAC Designed for Infection Control?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that hospital HVAC is designed for infection control by controlling where air goes, how fast it is replaced, and how well it is filtered. ASHRAE 170 sets the playbook room by room: air must move from clean to less-clean areas, so operating rooms stay positive to corridors while airborne infection isolation rooms stay negative, and each space gets a minimum air change rate with staged filtration that matches its risk. An operating room typically needs 20 total air changes per hour with 4 of outdoor air and MERV-16 final filtration; an isolation room needs 12 air changes with MERV-14 and direct exhaust outdoors.\n\nI design the pressure cascade before I size a single duct. Operating rooms sit at the top of the cascade — positive to every adjoining space — with supply air delivered from ceiling diffusers above the sterile field and low wall returns pulling air downward and away from the surgical zone. Protective environment rooms for immunocompromised patients are also positive, but with HEPA filtration and a tightly sealed envelope. Airborne infection isolation rooms reverse the logic: the room runs negative so air flows inward under the door, and an anteroom gives staff a buffer where the pressure steps down in stages. Continuous differential-pressure monitors at room entrances let staff see the relationship holding in real time.\n\nFiltration and environmental control do the rest of the work. Supply systems cascade from a prefilter to the final filter at or near each critical space, and HEPA filters protect operating rooms used for orthopedic, transplant, neurosurgery, and burn cases as well as all protective environment rooms. Temperature and relative humidity are held inside the ranges the standard sets for each space, because humidity control is both a comfort matter and an infection matter — too dry and static and respiratory irritation rise, too humid and microbial growth follows. I also plan for redundancy: fans, filters, and controls serving surgical and isolation spaces get the backup and monitoring that keeps them running through equipment failures.\n\nThe jurisdiction angle matters more in healthcare than anywhere else. In California, hospital HVAC designs pass through HCAI review with its own amendments, while most other states route through the department of health or a designated plan-review body — and local amendments can tighten filtration, monitoring, or redundancy beyond the national standard. I always confirm which edition of the ventilation standard the AHJ has adopted before locking room criteria, because designing to the wrong edition is the most expensive way to learn about a local amendment.",
