        heading: "Fume hood capture and containment",
        body: "Containment is the entire purpose of a fume hood, and it depends on more than fan size. I select hoods by hazard — standard chemical hoods for general work, and specialty hoods where the chemistry demands it, such as washdown hoods for perchloric acid that prevent explosive perchlorate salts from accumulating in the duct. Each hood gets an airflow monitor with a local alarm so the researcher knows immediately if capture is lost.\n\nPlacement is half the battle. Hoods go away from doors, busy aisles, and operable windows, because cross-drafts above a fraction of the face velocity will defeat even a well-sized hood. Supply diffusers are kept clear of the hood face, and I prefer ceiling diffusers with low throw near hoods over anything that jets air across the sash plane. During commissioning I witness containment testing with theatrical smoke or tracer gas at each hood in its as-installed condition — the factory test report is not proof of field performance.",
