        heading: "Manifolded versus dedicated exhaust",
        body: "A manifolded system collects hood and general exhaust into common risers served by a bank of parallel fans, with one fan typically held as standby. The advantages compound: a single tall stack with high discharge velocity disperses the plume above the roofline and away from intakes and operable windows, fan failure triggers automatic standby start instead of a lab shutdown, and maintenance happens at a few fan locations rather than dozens. The ductwork must be chemically compatible with everything the manifold carries and detailed so a failure in one branch cannot back-feed another.\n\nDedicated exhaust — one fan, one hood — trades that resilience for simplicity and isolation. It suits single-hood renovations, specialty hoods whose effluent cannot share ductwork, and buildings where phasing makes a manifold impractical. The penalties are real: many roof penetrations, no inherent redundancy, and low individual discharge velocities that demand careful stack design to avoid re-entrainment. Whichever architecture I choose, the discharge gets the same scrutiny — stack height, exit velocity, and distance from every intake on the building and its neighbors, verified against wind and dispersion guidance.",
