    description: "Commercial kitchen hood ventilation removes grease, heat, and smoke safely. How engineers size Type I and II hoods, makeup air, and grease duct routing.",
    h1: "How Is Commercial Kitchen Hood Ventilation Properly Designed?",
    answer: "The direct answer is that commercial kitchen hood ventilation is designed around capture and containment: hoods sized to the cooking equipment's heat and grease output pull contaminated air up and out, an equal volume of makeup air replaces it so the kitchen holds a slight negative to the dining room, and grease ductwork carries the exhaust safely through the building to a code-compliant discharge. The hood type follows the cooking — Type I grease hoods over anything producing grease-laden vapors, Type II hoods over heat-and-moisture appliances like dishwashers and ovens — and the whole system is governed by the mechanical code and NFPA 96 for fire protection.\n\nI start by mapping every cooking appliance to its duty: light, medium, heavy, or extra-heavy. That classification sets the exhaust rate per linear foot of hood, because a charbroiler needs far more capture airflow than a convection oven. Type I hoods get listed grease filters, stainless construction, and a fire suppression system tied to fuel shutoff; Type II hoods handle steam and heat with simpler construction. Hood geometry matters too — canopy hoods over island cooking lines need more airflow than wall-canopy hoods against a wall, since they lack the wall to help contain the plume.\n\nMakeup air is where kitchen designs most often fail. Exhausting thousands of cubic feet per minute without replacing it drives the kitchen deeply negative: exterior doors won't open, dining room air gets dragged through the kitchen carrying odors the wrong way, and hood capture collapses as air rushes past the cooking line. I provide dedicated makeup air — typically 80 to 90 percent of the exhaust volume, leaving the kitchen slightly negative to dining — introduced through perforated diffusers or a makeup air plenum at the hood so it doesn't create cross-drafts across the cooking surface. In hot climates the makeup air is cooled; the energy cost is real, but a kitchen that can't hold its pressure is a kitchen that can't pass inspection or keep staff.\n\nGrease duct routing gets the same care as the hood. Type I grease ducts are welded liquid-tight steel, routed as directly as possible to the exterior with cleanouts at every change of direction, enclosed in a fire-rated shaft where they pass through the building, and held clear of combustibles per code. The exhaust fan sits at the discharge end pulling the system negative — typically an upblast fan on the roof — so any duct leak draws room air in rather than pushing grease-laden air out into the building. Discharge goes up, away from intakes, operable windows, and property lines, and the fire suppression, fuel shutoff, and exhaust interlocks are tested as one system, not as separate trades' equipment.",
