        body: "I begin with the cooking equipment schedule, because the hood serves the equipment, not the other way around. Each appliance is classified by duty, and the exhaust rate per linear foot of hood follows that duty — extra-heavy equipment like solid-fuel charbroilers sits at the top of the scale. Hood length must overhang the equipment on all exposed sides so the thermal plume is captured before room air currents can peel it away, and island configurations get additional airflow to compensate for the missing wall.\n\nListed hoods carry their performance data with them, and I design to the listing rather than to rules of thumb. Demand-controlled kitchen ventilation — optic or temperature sensors that ramp exhaust and makeup air with actual cooking activity — cuts fan and conditioning energy dramatically in kitchens with variable loads, and I specify it wherever the budget allows. Every hood gets a fire suppression system listed for commercial cooking, interlocked to shut off fuel and power to the equipment it protects, because the suppression system and the hood are one life-safety assembly.",
