        heading: "Distribution, zone valves, and alarms",
        body: "The distribution network is engineered for isolation. Risers and branch piping are zoned so each clinical area — OR suite, ICU, ED, each nursing floor — can be valved off independently, with zone valve boxes placed outside the served area in staff-accessible locations. The piping itself is seamless copper tube cleaned for oxygen service, installed with nitrogen-purged brazing, protected from contamination during construction with capped ends, and routed to avoid damage and unauthorized access. Pressure drops are calculated so the most remote outlet still delivers full flow at usable pressure with the system at peak demand.\n\nAlarming mirrors the zoning. Area panels at nurse stations and in procedure areas monitor each gas in that zone; the master panel watches sources and main lines from a staffed engineering location. I design the alarm wiring and panel locations with the clinical staff — a panel nobody can see might as well not exist — and every alarm point gets tested during third-party verification. As-built drawings record every valve, outlet, and alarm location, because ten years later someone will need to isolate a zone at 2 a.m. and the drawings are the only map.",
