        body: "Outlet design is clinical coordination, not fixture counting. I walk the room types with the clinical team: the anesthesia workstation needs its gas complement at a reachable height, the surgical field needs vacuum and gases at the boom or headwall, patient rooms need oxygen, medical air, and vacuum at each bed position, and specialty areas — labor and delivery, cath labs, emergency — get their own configurations. Flow rates and simultaneous-use diversity set the branch sizing behind those outlets, so a fully occupied ICU at peak demand doesn't starve the farthest bed.\n\nThen verification proves it all. The independent verifier pressure-tests every system, blows down and purges the piping, tests particulate and purity, verifies every outlet for correct gas and cross-connection, tests every alarm point, and performance-tests the source equipment — producing the formal verification report the authority having jurisdiction and the accreditors require before patient use. I attend verification, resolve deficiencies with the contractor, and hand the owner a complete record set: verification report, as-builts, valve charts, and alarm schedules. That package is the system's birth certificate.\n\n• Categorize each system per NFPA 99 and design source redundancy for failure and service\n• Zone distribution with labeled valves outside each clinical area for emergency isolation\n• Monitor every zone with area alarms and sources with a master panel in a staffed location\n• Use gas-specific indexed connections and coordinate outlet placement with clinical staff\n• Require independent third-party verification with a formal report before patient use",
