        heading: "Pressure zoning and boosting",
        body: "The zone map is the first drawing I produce for a high-rise plumbing design. Starting from the municipal supply pressure at the service entrance and the building's elevations, I calculate the static pressure at every floor and draw zone boundaries where the pressure would exceed safe limits — typically keeping each zone's maximum under 80 psi at its lowest fixture. Upper zones may take street pressure directly through a single booster or even gravity; middle and lower zones get dedicated booster systems or step down through pressure-reducing valves from the zone above.\n\nBooster systems are packaged, variable-speed, and redundant: multiple pumps with automatic alternation and standby start, VFDs holding constant discharge pressure, and low-water and high-pressure safeties. I prefer taking suction from a break tank rather than directly from the municipal main where the water authority requires it — the tank decouples the building from main pressure fluctuations and gives the pumps a stable suction. Each zone gets its own pressure-reducing valve stations at branches where needed, with bypasses and isolation valves arranged so any valve can be serviced without draining the zone.",
